---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---
<Base
  title="About · Josh Approved"
  description="Why Josh Approved exists and how it works."
>
  <div class="brandrule"></div>
  <div class="container">
    <Nav variant="full" />

    <article class="prose">
      <h1>About</h1>
      <p class="lede">Hi — I'm Josh. I make small apps, and this is the honest version of why.</p>

      <p>
        I spent years building consumer apps inside the tech industry. I'm good at the
        thing those apps are designed to do: get you to come back, get you to tap, get
        you to upgrade. Up close, the pattern was always the same — take a tool that
        should take ten seconds, wrap it in an account so we can email you, bolt on an
        analytics SDK so we can watch what you do, and put the one feature you actually
        came for behind a subscription.
      </p>

      <p>
        I don't think most of the people doing this are villains. A lot of that
        instrumentation starts with a fair intention — you genuinely want to know where
        people get stuck so you can fix it. But the incentives only ever pull one way,
        and "understand the user" quietly becomes "increase the number." I'd shipped
        enough of it to know how that story ends.
      </p>

      <p>
        So in my own time I started making the opposite kind of app. A Josh Approved
        app does one thing, runs on your device, and then gets out of your way. No
        account unless the feature genuinely can't work without one. No analytics
        watching you use it. No ads. No "pro" tier hiding the part you needed. The whole
        app is the free app.
      </p>

      <p>
        It isn't a company. It's me, on the side, putting my name on things I'd be
        comfortable handing to my own family. That's what the little check means: I
        stand behind this one. If an app ever drifts — if it starts tracking you, or
        nagging you, or holding a feature hostage — it doesn't get to keep the badge,
        and I'd rather pull it than pretend.
      </p>

      <p>
        I also write about the patterns I'm pushing back on, in plain terms, trying to
        be fair to the other side — that's the <a href="/writing/">writing</a> section.
        If something here saved you a subscription and you want to throw a few dollars
        my way, you can. If you don't, the app is exactly the same. That's the deal,
        and it isn't going to change.
      </p>
    </article>

    <Footer />
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .brandrule {
    height: 3px;
    background: var(--marketing-terracotta);
  }
  .container {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 32px;
  }
  .prose {
    padding: 64px 0 32px;
  }
  .prose h1 {
    font-size: 48px;
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 16px;
  }
  .prose .lede {
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    margin: 0 0 24px;
  }
  .prose p {
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--fg);
    margin: 0 0 16px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 540px) {
    .container { padding: 0 20px; }
    .prose h1 { font-size: 36px; }
    .prose { padding: 40px 0 24px; }
  }
</style>
